/* THE INSTALLER'S HALF of the extension lifecycle — discover and install — and a page that did not exist.
 *
 * Everything on it was previously written inside the PUBLISHING guide, under headings addressed to an author:
 * "what the person installing sees", "updates". So the reader doing the most common thing anybody does with an
 * extension had to open a page about registry pull requests to find out what the dialog in front of them meant.
 * Authoring is the other half, and it is /api.
 *
 * WRITTEN AS A DECISION, not as a feature list. The order is the order the reader meets it: where these come
 * from, what the dialog is telling them, how to read a listing, then living with one — updates, withdrawal,
 * money. Every section that describes a protection also says what it does not protect against, because the one
 * failure this page could cause is a reader installing something on the strength of a feeling. */

const contributions = [
    { line: `Adds a tile to your rail`, means: `A screen of its own, in the left-hand strip, alongside the built-in ones.` },
    { line: `Reads your workspace logs`, means: `A specific daemon route it may call. Anything not on the list is refused at runtime.` },
    { line: `Runs a background process`, means: `Something that keeps running while the sandbox does, supervised and restartable.` },
    { line: `Puts a tool on your agent's PATH`, means: `A command the agent can run in its shell, the same way it runs git.` },
    { line: `Adds a connector`, means: `A card in your Capabilities catalog, with its own config form and credential.` },
    { line: `Bakes layers into your image`, means: `Real packages installed in the sandbox, applied when you rebuild the environment.` },
];

const policies = [
    {
        policy: `Notify`,
        what: `The badge waits for you. Nothing changes until you click.`,
        who: `The default, and the right setting for anything touching real credentials.`,
    },
    {
        policy: `Agent-prepared`,
        what: `Your own agent reads the diff between the two commits the moment a release is listed.`,
        who: `Deciding then means opening a finished review instead of starting one.`,
    },
    {
        policy: `Auto`,
        what: `Applied unattended — but only when four things hold at once, and it reverts itself when they stop.`,
        who: `Extensions you'd rather not think about, from listings somebody has actually read.`,
    },
];
---

<DocsLayout
    pageId="extensions"
    heading="Extensions"
    lead="Most of what your sandbox can do arrives as an extension — including the parts that shipped with it. This is how to find one, read what it may touch before you say yes, and stay in control of it afterwards."
>
    <h2>What you're installing</h2>
    <p>
        An extension is one package that can add several kinds of thing at once: a screen in your rail, a connector card in your
        capabilities, a command-line tool for the agent, a background process, or all of them together. That is deliberate — you install a
        <em>product</em> ("deployments", "knowledge", "Reddit research"), not a skill here and a view there, so it is one identity, one trust
        decision, and one switch that unwires all of it.
    </p>
    <p>
        Most of the product you already use is extensions — over twenty-five of them. Activity, Automations, Memory, Knowledge, Deployments,
        Pipelines, the file viewers, the commit graph, the Discord, Slack, Telegram, WhatsApp and IMAP gateways, the Google Workspace
        connection, the connector catalog. They're built against the same public interface a third-party one uses, which is worth knowing for
        a practical reason rather than a philosophical one: there is no privileged internal path, so a third-party extension can be as good as
        a built-in one.
    </p>

    <h2>Where they come from</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Already there.</strong> First-party extensions ship inside the image. You can switch them off; you can't remove them, and
            they don't need installing.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The gallery.</strong> The <a href="/extensions/">extensions page</a> lists everything published to the official registry,
            and the same list is browsable inside the app at <strong>Capabilities → Add → Extension</strong>. One click installs from there.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A repo URL.</strong> No registry required. Paste a git URL and a commit into the same dialog and it installs — which is how
            a company runs internal extensions that are published nowhere.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Your own workspace.</strong> A directory you (or your agent) wrote under <code>.intentic/workspace-extensions/</code> runs
            straight from where it sits. No clone, no install, no publishing.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        All four land in the same list on <strong>Sandbox → Extensions</strong>, with one on/off switch each — so "what is running in here" is
        one screen regardless of how any of it arrived.
    </p>

    <h2>What the install dialog is telling you</h2>
    <p>
        Extension code runs in your browser and on your machine, so the install makes its declared host integrations legible
        <em>before</em> approval. The dialog is the extension's manifest rendered as plain English — one line per thing it declared, with the
        exact daemon routes it may call through the extension API spelled out underneath:
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>A line like this</th><th>Means</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {contributions.map((row) => (<tr><td>{row.line}</td><td>{row.means}</td></tr>))}
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The host enforces those declarations, and the three sentences worth remembering are:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>An extension that declared no routes <strong>cannot call the daemon through the extension API</strong>.</li>
        <li>An extension that declared no badge <strong>cannot interrupt you</strong> from a screen you aren't looking at.</li>
        <li>An extension that registers a view its manifest never declared is <strong>refused at runtime</strong>, not warned about.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        The extension API injects transport authorization, and a secret setting reads back as "still set" rather than as its value. That
        reduces routine credential handling; it is not a secrecy or confinement boundary. A browser bundle shares the app's JavaScript realm
        and can reach its DOM, storage, browser globals and ordinary network APIs, while process code runs on the sandbox machine.
    </p>

    <h3>You approve a commit, not a project</h3>
    <p>
        Installing from anywhere but your own workspace takes a full 40-character commit sha, never a branch. The code you read about is the
        code that runs; if the author force-pushes their branch tomorrow, nothing on your machine changes. It also means an update is always an
        explicit, visible act — there is no mechanism by which the code quietly becomes different code.
    </p>
    <p>
        Your own workspace extensions are pinned to nothing, for the same reason your own repositories aren't: there is no upstream that could
        change them behind your back, only files you can read.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading a listing before you install</h2>
    <p>
        Every listing carries one word about how much anybody has actually checked it, and the difference between them matters more than any
        other signal on the card:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Listed</strong> — in the official registry, the exact source passed the deterministic scan and adversarial agent audit. No human source review is claimed.</li>
        <li><strong>Verified</strong> — somebody read the source at that exact commit. A newer commit is unverified again until re-read.</li>
        <li><strong>Blocked</strong> — known-malicious or known-broken, with the reason recorded for installed-extension advisories.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        A separate <strong>✓ loads</strong> mark means a nightly job re-checked the pinned commit and it would install. That is evidence, not
        endorsement — "it loads" and "somebody read it" are different claims by different parties, and the gallery keeps them apart. Stars are
        just the source repo's stars.
    </p>
    <p>
        <a href="/api/verify/">Verification &amp; trust</a> is precise about what each of those words guarantees and, more usefully, what none
        of them do. The short version: installing an extension is trusting its author, the same as installing an editor plugin. What you get
        here is source-bound automated review plus a declaration of cooperative host access. Those reduce risk; they do not isolate extension
        code from the app or sandbox.
    </p>

    <h2>Living with one</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>Sandbox → Extensions</strong> is the whole management surface. Each row has a switch, and switching one off unwires it
        everywhere at once: no view, no command, no PATH entry, no connector card, no environment variable, no background process. Nothing is
        left half-connected, which is why "turn it off and see" is a reasonable first move when something is behaving oddly.
    </p>
    <p>
        Settings — including secrets and any environment variables the extension asked the agent for — are stored by the daemon under the
        extension's identity rather than inside its checkout. They survive an update, a revert, and even a remove followed by a re-add.
    </p>

    <h2>Updates</h2>
    <p>
        Because what you installed is a commit and a listing carries one, "there's a newer version" is a comparison your sandbox makes for
        itself: daily, and whenever you have the Extensions tab open. A newer pinned commit badges the row. What happens when you click depends
        on one thing only — whether the new version wants more than the old one did:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Same powers, new code</strong>: one click. The new version is cloned into staging and validated first, the outgoing
            version's processes are stopped, and the swap only happens if the new one is sound. A broken release cannot replace a working
            install, and nothing is left running the version you replaced.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>It wants more than before</strong> — a new route, a new process, a new PATH entry, a badge it didn't have — and the update
            <strong>asks again</strong>. The dialog computes the difference for you and states every addition as a plain sentence. Approval
            never transfers to powers you didn't approve. You can also ask your own agent to read the code diff between the two commits before
            deciding.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        After a swap the daemon watches for a minute that what the new version declared actually came up. The version it replaced is kept one
        back, so <strong>revert is one click</strong> — after a failed health check, or simply because the update made things worse.
    </p>

    <h3>Nothing updates itself unless you say so</h3>
    <p>
        On a machine you own, silently changing the code that runs against your repos and credentials is not a convenience. So the default is
        that nothing moves without you, and you raise individual extensions up a ladder as you come to trust them:
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Policy</th><th>What it does</th><th>When it fits</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {
                policies.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>{row.policy}</strong></td>
                        <td>{row.what}</td>
                        <td>{row.who}</td>
                    </tr>
                ))
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        The four conditions for <strong>Auto</strong>, all required together: the listing is human-verified, the powers didn't grow, validation
        passes, and the health watch stays green afterwards. Any one of them failing stops the update, or undoes it.
    </p>

    <h3>When a listing is withdrawn</h3>
    <p>
        The same daily comparison protects people who already installed something, not only people browsing. A listing marked
        <strong>blocked</strong> raises an advisory on your installed row carrying the registry's recorded reason, and by default the extension
        is <strong>switched off</strong>. That is the one thing that happens without asking you, because disabling runs no new code and reverses
        with a click.
    </p>
    <p>
        The mirror case: a release flagged as a <strong>security fix</strong> promotes its update badge from ambient to loud. There the version
        you already have is the dangerous one, so waiting is the risky choice rather than the safe one.
    </p>

    <h2>Premium extensions</h2>
    <p>
        Almost everything is free — free to publish, free to install, with no cut taken from anyone. A publisher may mark a single extension
        <strong>premium</strong>, and both the gallery and the app badge it before you click. Installing one needs a monthly membership and
        <strong>donates a published number of your credits to its creator</strong>: once on install, and at most monthly on updates.
    </p>
    <p>
        Nothing about it is metered. No usage is counted, reported or telemetered anywhere — the deliberate act of choosing to install is the
        entire signal, which is also why nobody can farm it. <a href="/api/earn/">The creator pool</a> has the arithmetic and the public ledger.
    </p>

